4. Requirements — FIXED
Functional — the System Model Set must contain all of the following:

F1. MODEL SELECTION. For each of the four perspectives, decide REQUIRED,
    PARTIAL, or NOT NEEDED, with a one-line justification tied to a
    project property from the PAR or SRS — not to habit or to UML's
    completeness as a notation:
      - Context — required for essentially every system; state only what
        would make it skippable (a component with no external boundary at
        all is rare enough to name explicitly if claimed).
      - Interaction — required whenever the system has actors external to
        it (human or system); the depth (use cases only, vs. use cases
        plus sequence diagrams for select interactions) scales with
        interaction complexity, not with system size.
      - Structural — required whenever the domain has entities with
        persistent state, relationships, or a class hierarchy worth
        naming; a stateless transformation pipeline may need little beyond
        its data model.
      - Behavioral — select data-driven modeling (activity diagrams over a
        processing pipeline) for systems organized around data transformation;
        select state-machine modeling (state diagrams) for systems that are
        event-driven, reactive, or have modes with different permitted
        behavior; select both if the system genuinely has both kinds of
        subsystem, and say which part gets which.
    A perspective marked NOT NEEDED without a stated reason is a defect.

F2. PROVENANCE CARRY-FORWARD. Every model element — every class, every
    actor, every message, every state, every transition, every context
    boundary — cites the SRS requirement id it represents and inherits
    that requirement's provenance label from the index. An element with no
    citable requirement is itself labeled MODEL-PROPOSED (parallel to
    CH4's PROPOSED, but flagged as originating here, not upstream) and is
    never rendered indistinguishably from an element that traces to a
    GIVEN requirement. Where a diagram would look incomplete or
    unbalanced without an uncited element, leave it incomplete and say so
    — an honest gap is a finding, not a defect, exactly as CH4 established.

F3. CONTEXT MODEL. Show the system boundary: what lies inside, what lies
    outside, and every external system, actor, or data/control flow
    crossing that boundary. State explicitly where the boundary was a
    technical necessity versus a social or organizational choice — the
    same activity might sit inside or outside the system depending on who
    owns it, not on what the software could technically do. If
    `{{EXISTING_SYSTEM_DETAIL}}` is populated, produce a brief AS-IS
    context model alongside the TO-BE one, and label each accordingly. Do
    not blend them into a single unlabeled diagram.

F4. INTERACTION MODELS (if selected). Produce use case diagrams naming
    every actor and use case with a one-line description each, sourced to
    an SRS requirement. For sequence diagrams, do not attempt exhaustive
    coverage of every interaction — that is wasted effort under N5's
    proportionality rule. Select interactions for sequence-diagram detail
    using this priority order: (a) any interaction the SRS flagged as a
    negotiation item or conflict, (b) any interaction involving a BLOCKED
    or high-risk PROPOSED/DOMAIN-ASSUMED requirement, (c) any interaction
    whose ordering, concurrency, or error handling is not obvious from the
    use case description alone. State which interactions were left at the
    use-case level and why sequence detail wasn't warranted for them.

F5. STRUCTURAL MODELS (if selected). Produce a class diagram of the
    problem domain — classes, attributes, key operations, associations,
    generalization (inheritance) hierarchies, and aggregation ("part-of")
    relationships. This is a domain model, not a design: it represents
    entities and relationships the requirements establish must exist, not
    decisions about how the system will be built internally. If a
    plausible domain class has no requirement behind it, label it
    MODEL-PROPOSED per F2 rather than silently including it because the
    diagram looks more complete with it. Where the SRS distinguishes
    enduring versus volatile requirements, note which structural elements
    rest on volatile ground and are more likely to need rework.

F6. BEHAVIORAL MODELS (if selected). For data-driven modeling, produce
    activity diagrams showing the sequence of processing steps, decision
    points, and data flow through them, each step sourced to a system
    requirement. For state-machine modeling, produce state diagrams naming
    every state, the events that trigger transitions, and the guard
    conditions where relevant; every state must be reachable and every
    transition must correspond to a stated system requirement or be
    flagged MODEL-PROPOSED. Where the PAR or SRS indicates safety or
    reliability criticality, explicitly check for and name any state from
    which no recovery transition exists, and route that finding rather
    than silently modeling a dead end as acceptable.

F7. AS-IS / TO-BE DISCIPLINE. Wherever an existing system is modeled
    alongside the planned one (per F3), keep every model — not only the
    context model — clearly labeled AS-IS or TO-BE. A structural or
    behavioral model that mixes current and planned elements without
    labeling them is a defect; a reader must be able to tell what exists
    today from what is being proposed.

F8. MODEL PURPOSE AND COMPLETENESS LEVEL. State explicitly, once, which of
    three purposes this model set serves: supporting discussion with
    stakeholders (incomplete and even locally incorrect models are
    acceptable here), documenting an existing or delivered system
    (accurate but not necessarily exhaustive), or driving implementation
    or code generation (must be both correct and complete). Derive this
    from the PAR's detail level and the project's phase; do not default to
    the highest completeness level out of caution, since that manufactures
    the same false confidence F2 exists to prevent, just at the level of
    the whole model set rather than one element.

F9. MODEL-DRIVEN ENGINEERING DECISION. State whether this project warrants
    model-driven engineering — building platform-independent models formal
    and complete enough to generate code or a platform-specific model from
    them. This is justified only where the PAR indicates a stable,
    well-understood domain, sufficient scale to amortize the modeling
    investment, and (per `{{DPC}}` if available) tooling that can actually
    consume the models generatively. Where these do not hold — the common
    case — say plainly that MDE is not warranted and that the models in
    this document serve discussion and documentation purposes per F8, not
    code generation. Do not adopt MDE because UML tooling exists; tooling
    availability is necessary, not sufficient.

F10. CROSS-MODEL VALIDATION. Check the model set for internal consistency
     and report per check, naming the specific elements that fail:
       - Every actor in an interaction model appears in the context model.
       - Every class referenced in a sequence diagram appears in the
         structural model, or is external and marked as such.
       - Every state in a behavioral model corresponds to a condition
         describable from the SRS, and every transition to a stated event
         or action.
       - No model element contradicts another (e.g., a class diagram
         cardinality that a sequence diagram's message pattern violates).
     Where a check fails, do not silently repair it by editing one model to
     match the other; report the mismatch and, if it traces back to an
     ambiguity in the SRS itself rather than an error introduced here,
     raise `REQUIREMENT_AMBIGUOUS`.

F11. OPEN QUESTIONS SURFACED BY MODELING. Modeling routinely exposes
     ambiguity that prose did not — two readers of a sequence diagram
     disagreeing about message order is a sharper test than two readers of
     a sentence. List every such finding: the requirement id, what the
     model revealed, and whether it is severe enough to raise
     `REQUIREMENT_AMBIGUOUS` now or minor enough to note and continue.

F12. MODEL INDEX. Emit the machine-readable block specified in Section 7.

Non-functional:

N1. Every model element traces to a requirement id or is labeled
    MODEL-PROPOSED — no exceptions, no silent omissions.
N2. Domain neutrality applies to the prompt, not the output; this model set
    is necessarily specific to the project given.
N3. No fabrication rendered as certainty. A model is not a place to
    resolve an ambiguity CH4 left open; render the ambiguity, don't
    smooth it into a single confident interpretation.
N4. Notation discipline. Use UML conventions throughout, rendered as
    structured text (PlantUML/Mermaid-compatible), stated once, applied
    consistently. Note any deliberate deviation.
N5. Proportionality. Model only what F1 selected, at the completeness
    level F8 states. A complete formal model set for a project that needed
    a discussion aid is waste, not thoroughness.
5. Constraints — FIXED
- Do not draw a model element that has no requirement behind it without
  labeling it MODEL-PROPOSED. This is the primary failure mode of this
  stage, parallel to CH4's primary failure mode, and it is more dangerous
  here because a diagram is more persuasive than a sentence.
- Do not resolve an ambiguity the SRS left open by picking the more
  convenient reading so the diagram looks clean. Render the ambiguity or
  raise it upstream.
- Do not design system architecture, allocate components to subsystems,
  choose data storage technology, or specify algorithms. A domain class
  diagram is not a design; if you find yourself deciding how something is
  implemented rather than what the domain requires to be true, you have
  left your scope — that belongs to CH6.
- Do not produce a fully formal, implementation-ready model set by default.
  Match F8's stated purpose; over-completeness is not free.
- Do not adopt model-driven engineering because the tooling exists or
  because it is presented as best practice. Justify it against F9's
  criteria or state plainly that it is not warranted.
- Do not blend AS-IS and TO-BE elements in a single unlabeled model.
- Do not attempt exhaustive sequence-diagram coverage of every
  interaction. Select per F4's priority order and say what was left out
  and why.
- On re-entry, revise the prior model set and report the diff. Do not
  regenerate from scratch.
- If `{{SRS}}` or `{{PAR}}` is missing or contains `halt: true`, emit HALT
  naming the missing artifact.
6. Process — FIXED
Work in this order:

1. Read the PAR for detail level and MDE relevance, and the SRS's
   requirements index in full before drawing anything. Build a working
   list of which requirement ids exist and their provenance labels — this
   is what F2's carry-forward discipline draws from.

2. Select perspectives (F1) before modeling anything. Justify each
   selection against a stated project property.

3. Model in this order: context first (it fixes the boundary everything
   else operates inside), then structural (it fixes the vocabulary the
   other models use), then interaction, then behavioral. Cite a
   requirement id for every element as you draw it — do not draw first and
   attach citations afterward, which produces the same optimistic-labeling
   drift CH4 warned against in its own audit pass.

4. Cross-validate (F10) against the SRS and across models.

5. Self-critique before finalizing. Check specifically: does any model
   element lack a citation or a MODEL-PROPOSED label; does any diagram
   look more complete or more certain than the SRS requirements underneath
   it justify; has AS-IS/TO-BE labeling been kept consistent; has any
   design decision crept into a domain model.

6. Adversarial pass. Pick the single model element most likely to be
   MODEL-PROPOSED dressed up as GIVEN. State how you would prove it either
   way, and whether you actually did that check on it or are asserting it.

Do not ask clarifying questions mid-run. Record findings in F11 and
continue.

HUMAN-IN-THE-LOOP VARIANT (interactive invocation only): after step 2,
present the perspective selection and justification alone and stop for
confirmation before modeling.
