### 8. Verification — FIXED

Structural gates (router-checkable): V1. All eleven sections present under the exact specified headings. V2. Section 10 parses as valid YAML; project_id matches the PAR and SRS. V3. Every model element in every diagram appears in that diagram's element citation table. V4. Every element citation table entry has a provenance value; none are blank. V5. Every perspective marked "required" or "partial" in Section 1 has a corresponding non-empty section; every "not-needed" has a stated reason. V6. mde_adopted: true requires a non-empty mde_rationale addressing all three F9 criteria explicitly. V7. Every AS-IS model is labeled distinctly from every TO-BE model.

Content gates (reviewer-checkable): V8. Spot-check five elements labeled "given" or "derived" against the SRS's requirements index. Any that cannot be located is a provenance failure and the whole model set's labeling is then suspect — mirrors CH4's V11. V9. No domain model in Section 5 contains an implementation or storage decision (a table name, a specific data structure, a technology choice). V10. Sequence diagrams in Section 4 cover only interactions justified by F4's priority order; any that don't must be explained or removed. V11. The adversarial pass in the transcript names a specific model element, not a category. V12. model_purpose is consistent with the actual completeness of the models — a "discussion" purpose paired with fully elaborated, production-complete models is a mismatch worth flagging.

Generalization gate (applied when this prompt is graded as an artifact): V13. Run against an SRS whose confirmed_fraction (from CH4) is low. The resulting model set's provenance distribution should visibly reflect that — a high proportion of "proposed"/"domain-assumed"/ "model-proposed" elements, not a confident-looking diagram that erases the uncertainty it was built from. A model set that looks more certain than its source SRS has failed regardless of diagram quality. V14. Run against three projects with different dominant system types (an interactive product, a data-processing pipeline, a reactive/embedded system). The perspective selection in Section 1 must differ materially across the three. Identical perspective selection regardless of system type is evidence the prompt is drawing UML reflexively rather than selecting.


