### Design decisions worth defending in the writeup

**F2's provenance carry-forward is the chapter's whole argument, restated in a more
dangerous medium.** CH4 established that an LLM's failure mode is not refusing to
answer — it's answering beautifully from nothing. A diagram is a *more* beautiful,
*more* confident-looking answer than a paragraph, built from the same possible
nothing. Without F2, everything CH4 built to keep the specification honest gets
silently overwritten the moment it's redrawn as a box.

**F8 (model purpose) prevents the opposite failure — manufactured rigor.** Sommerville
is explicit that incomplete, even locally incorrect models are fine when the job is
supporting discussion. An agent given no other instruction will default to maximum
completeness because that looks more competent. Forcing an explicit purpose
declaration, derived from the PAR rather than from the model's own preference, is the
proportionality discipline this whole library keeps re-applying at each new stage.

**F9 is deliberately biased toward "not warranted."** Model-driven engineering is
expensive relative to its payoff outside large, stable, well-tooled projects. An
agent asked whether to use MDE, like an agent asked whether to use agile practices in
CH3, will reach for the more sophisticated-sounding answer unless the prompt
specifically requires three named criteria to hold first.

**F11 closes a loop CH4 opened.** CH4 declared `REQUIREMENT_AMBIGUOUS` as a trigger it
can receive; this is the first prompt that actually raises it, using diagrams as a
sharper ambiguity detector than prose review. That a sequence diagram can surface an
ordering ambiguity two people would read past in a sentence is a genuinely useful
property of modeling, not just a formality — it's worth stating plainly in review
rather than treating the loop-back as boilerplate.

### Known generalization risks

- **Model-element citation will drift the same way CH4's provenance labels drifted.**
  Expect MODEL-PROPOSED elements to start appearing uncited, or GIVEN elements to
  appear without a real citation, as diagrams grow. The self-critique step and V8
  exist for exactly this and should be watched closely in review.
- **Perspective selection may default to "produce all four" out of caution rather
  than genuine selection.** V14's generalization gate is the direct test; if three
  very different system types all get the same four-perspective treatment, the
  selection logic in F1 isn't actually running.
- **The sourcing gap noted at the top of this file is itself a live risk for the
  library, not just a caveat.** If this table's section numbers turn out to be wrong
  once checked against the real text, that's a finding worth reporting the same way
  CH4 wants provenance failures reported — not quietly corrected without comment.